<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Efficiency Analysis</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="A1.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">PTQTP’s hardware efficiency is consistent with the binary quantization method. Moreover, the inherent sparsity of trit-plane offers potential opportunities for further quantization to reduce storage requirements and acceleration on application-specific integrated circuit (ASIC).</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Hardware Efficiency</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PTQTP framework leverages ternary operations <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve hardware-efficient computations. For a ternary element <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{m}\in\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{m}\in\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and scaling coefficient <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the multiplication operation <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\cdot c_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\cdot c_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> can be implemented as an addition, eliminating floating-point multipliers and replacing them with sign flips, identity mappings, or zeroing operations. This reduces the arithmetic intensity to <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> per element in the matrices. The far lower multiplication and the inherent sparsity in PTQTP than other low-bit methods (i.e., 2-bits to 8-bits) and the decomposition into two trit-planes <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{(1)},T^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{(1)},T^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing parallelized additive superposition: <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{W}=\text{diag}(\alpha^{(1)})T^{(1)}+\text{diag}(\alpha^{(2)})T^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{W}=\text{diag}(\alpha^{(1)})T^{(1)}+\text{diag}(\alpha^{(2)})T^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each plane can be processed independently on parallelized architectures, exploiting data-level parallelism and minimizing latency in inference acceleration. This bandwidth reduction is critical for latency-bound applications, as memory access often dominates inference time in modern hardware architectures, though there are two parallel trit-planes to represent the original weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Computation and Time Complexity of Quantization</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total Computational Complexity:</span> For row <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the normal equation matrix <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=(S_{i})^{T}S_{i}+\lambda_{i}I_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=(S_{i})^{T}S_{i}+\lambda_{i}I_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math>, with inverse computed in constant time <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> using the adjugate formula:</p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{-1}=\frac{1}{\det(A_{i})}\begin{bmatrix}A_{i}(2,2)&amp;-A_{i}{(1,2)}\\
-A_{i}(2,1)&amp;A_{i}(1,1)\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{-1}=\frac{1}{\det(A_{i})}\begin{bmatrix}A_{i}(2,2)&amp;-A_{i}{(1,2)}\\
-A_{i}(2,1)&amp;A_{i}(1,1)\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(A_{i})=A_{i}(1,1)A_{i}(2,2)-A_{i}(1,2)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(A_{i})=A_{i}(1,1)A_{i}(2,2)-A_{i}(1,2)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The vector <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}=S_{i}^{T}W_{i}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}=S_{i}^{T}W_{i}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math> operations (dot products for each column of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), leading to <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> per row. Adding up to <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rows, this step is <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math> per iteration. In addition, for each element <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, evaluating <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{(1)},T^{(2)})\in\{-1,0,1\}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{(1)},T^{(2)})\in\{-1,0,1\}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> involves <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> arithmetic operations per element, resulting in <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> complexity per element. For <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> elements per row, this is <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rows per iteration. Therefore, with <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, the total computational complexity is <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T_{\text{max}}\cdot nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T_{\text{max}}\cdot nd)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total Time Complexity</span>: For each iteration, the time complexity can be presented as follows:</p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{\mathcal{O}(nd)}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{Ridge Regression}\\
\text{solve for }\alpha\end{subarray}}+\underbrace{\mathcal{O}(nd)}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{Trit-Plane}\\
\text{element-wise search}\end{subarray}}=\mathcal{O}(nd);\underbrace{\mathcal{O}(n)}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{Convergence}\\
\text{Check}\end{subarray}}\ll\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext>Ridge Regression</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>solve for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext>Trit-Plane</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>element-wise search</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext>Convergence</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>Check</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{\mathcal{O}(nd)}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{Ridge Regression}\\
\text{solve for }\alpha\end{subarray}}+\underbrace{\mathcal{O}(nd)}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{Trit-Plane}\\
\text{element-wise search}\end{subarray}}=\mathcal{O}(nd);\underbrace{\mathcal{O}(n)}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{Convergence}\\
\text{Check}\end{subarray}}\ll\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With empirical convergence in <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{max}}\leq 50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{max}}\leq 50</annotation></semantics></math> iterations (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Convergence Analysis ‣ PTQTP: Post-Training Quantization to Trit-Planes for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>), the total time complexity is <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(T_{\text{max}}\cdot nd\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(T_{\text{max}}\cdot nd\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, exhibiting linear scalability with the dimension of weight matrix <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,d</annotation></semantics></math>, critical for deployment on LLMs with billions of parameters. <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T_{\text{max}}\cdot nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T_{\text{max}}\cdot nd)</annotation></semantics></math> complexity is optimal for low-bit quantization when considering both approximation quality and computational efficiency. Compared to PTQ methods like GPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and AWQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PTQTP achieves lower per-iteration complexity due to its closed-form solutions and fixed-dimensional ridge regression.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For example, GPTQ’s iterative Hessian-based optimization has a per-iteration complexity of <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which becomes infeasible for large <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. The experiment results in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ PTQTP: Post-Training Quantization to Trit-Planes for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show that PTQTP can stably converges in <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>50 iterations with different model structures, resulting in 1.2-28.79 <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup during quantization on modern GPUs, which are shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ PTQTP: Post-Training Quantization to Trit-Planes for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16989v1/Ternary_values.png" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/239;" width="548" height="239" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The single trit-plane update process during the optimization iterations on LLaMA3.1-8B.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Space Complexity and Memory Saving</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Space Complexity.</span>
A matrix <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> in FP16 format requires <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2nd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2nd</annotation></semantics></math> bytes (2 bytes per element).
However, each ternary element <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{m}\in\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{m}\in\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> can be encoded with <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> bits (since <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq 2^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq 2^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), thus two trit-planes stored by <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nd/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nd/2</annotation></semantics></math> bytes. Moreover, two vectors <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{(1)},\alpha^{(2)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(1)},\alpha^{(2)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in FP16 require <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4n</annotation></semantics></math> bytes. Therefore, the total storage complexity can be presented as <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math>, achieving a compression ratio of <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> for the trit-plane relative to FP16.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, for a typical LLM layer with <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1024</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4096</annotation></semantics></math>, PTQTP reduces storage from 8MB to 1.004MB (i.e., 0.5 MB for trit-planes and 0.504 MB for scaling coefficients), illustrating a <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="7.96\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.96</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.96\times</annotation></semantics></math> compression ratio. This reduction enables deploying LLMs on devices with limited memory, such as edge GPUs with 8 GB RAM or mobile devices with 4 GB RAM. The optimized trit-planes examples during the iteration progress are illustrated in Fig. <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ A.2 Computation and Time Complexity of Quantization ‣ Appendix A Efficiency Analysis ‣ PTQTP: Post-Training Quantization to Trit-Planes for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
